bmatrix}8 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186237266 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186237279 :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186237317 :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186237348 :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186237354 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-2y-2z \\\\ 4+z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-2y-2z \\\\ 4+z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186237395 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186237399 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186237436 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186237775 :question/problem "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]=$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186237775 :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6+(-2) & (-1)+9 \\\\ 7+1 & 0+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6+(-2) & (-1)+9 \\\\ 7+1 & 0+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237776 :question/problem "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y+2z=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & a & 2 \\\\ b & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y+2z=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & a & 2 \\\\ b & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & c\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186237776 :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0x+y+2z=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,\\,b=1,\\,c=1.$" "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0x+y+2z=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,\\,b=1,\\,c=1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237787 :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y+z=1 \\\\ 3y-z=-2 \\\\ -3x+4y-2z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7(-6+4)-2(-3)+1(9) \\\\  & =-14+6+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ -8 & -11 & 34 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 21\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -34 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 21\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 77\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-18,\\,y=25,\\,z=77.$" "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y+z=1 \\\\ 3y-z=-2 \\\\ -3x+4y-2z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7(-6+4)-2(-3)+1(9) \\\\  & =-14+6+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ -8 & -11 & 34 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -34 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 77\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-18,\\,y=25,\\,z=77.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237803 :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=-1 \\\\ 4y+5z=0 \\\\ x+6z=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(24)-2(-5)+3(0-4) \\\\  & =24+10-12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 12 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}24 & 5 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}-30 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{11} \\\\ -\\frac{10}{11} \\\\ \\frac{8}{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{15}{11},y=-\\frac{10}{11},z=\\frac{8}{11}.$" "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=-1 \\\\ 4y+5z=0 \\\\ x+6z=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(24)-2(-5)+3(0-4) \\\\  & =24+10-12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 12 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 5 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}-30 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{15}{11} \\\\ -\\frac{10}{11} \\\\ \\frac{8}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{15}{11},y=-\\frac{10}{11},z=\\frac{8}{11}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237804 :question/worked-solution "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z=5 \\\\ 2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ -2x-2y+z=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1+4)+1(2+4)+3(-4+2) \\\\  & =5+6-6 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -\\frac{19}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=3,y=-\\frac{19}{5},z=-\\frac{3}{5}.$" "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z=5 \\\\ 2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ -2x-2y+z=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1+4)+1(2+4)+3(-4+2) \\\\  & =5+6-6 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -\\frac{19}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=3,y=-\\frac{19}{5},z=-\\frac{3}{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237805 :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-12+10)+2(32-35)+3(-16+21) \\\\  & =-8-6+15 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ -49 \\\\ -58\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=19,y=-49,z=-58.$" "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-12+10)+2(32-35)+3(-16+21) \\\\  & =-8-6+15 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 \\\\ -49 \\\\ -58\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=19,y=-49,z=-58.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237810 :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(7-2)-3(-2)+1(4-21) \\\\  & =10+6-17 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & -17 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -17 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-29 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 100\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-29,y=-12,z=100.$" "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(7-2)-3(-2)+1(4-21) \\\\  & =10+6-17 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -17 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -17 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-29 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 100\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-29,y=-12,z=100.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237812 :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1-3(-2))+0-1(-2-3) \\\\  & =1+6+2+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{2}{3},y=-\\frac{2}{3},z=\\frac{2}{3}.$" "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1-3(-2))+0-1(-2-3) \\\\  & =1+6+2+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{2}{3},y=-\\frac{2}{3},z=\\frac{2}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237813 :question/problem "Given that $c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $c.$" "Given that $c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $c.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237813 :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,c\\,| & =\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $c$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}c \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,c\\,| & =\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $c$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}c \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237814 :question/worked-solution "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2(2)+2(1) \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 4 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}.$" "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2(2)+2(1) \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 4 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237827 :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$" "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2x\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237828 :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." "Find the angle between $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186237830 :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "Find the angle between $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186237831 :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "Find the angle between $a=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186237848 :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b.$" "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237870 :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237886 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237886 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=\\sqrt{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}]=1+\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-1 \\\\ 1+\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\sqrt{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}=1+\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}-1 \\\\ 1+\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237887 :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-6 & 2y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-6=0 \\\\ 2y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2y-2=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,1).$" "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-6 & 2y-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-6=0 \\\\ 2y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2y-2=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186237888 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237888 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4+0+3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{2}=5\\cdot 7=35.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4+0+3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{2}=5\\cdot 7=35.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237889 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237889 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-4)+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{(-4)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-8}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}=5\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5})=-7.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-4)+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{(-4)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-8}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}=5\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5})=-7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237890 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $6{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $6{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237890 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 4+8\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-36+16}{16+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20}{20} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}=6\\cdot (-1)=-6.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 4+8\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-36+16}{16+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20}{20} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}=6\\cdot (-1)=-6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237891 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $4{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $4{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237891 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot (-5)+(-12)\\cdot 4}{(-5)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-75-48}{25+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{123}{41} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}=4\\cdot (-3)=-12.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot (-5)+(-12)\\cdot 4}{(-5)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-75-48}{25+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{123}{41} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}=4\\cdot (-3)=-12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237893 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $100{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $100{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237893 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 25+0\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-4)}{{25}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-12}{625+9+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{650} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n100{x}_{2}=100\\cdot \\frac{1}{50}=2.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 25+0\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-4)}{{25}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-12}{625+9+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{650} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n100{x}_{2}=100\\cdot \\frac{1}{50}=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237917 :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $C={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $C={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186237917 :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $C\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $C\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186237919 :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- **II.** $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- **III.** $B={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply,