\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186237920 :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- **II.** $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- **III.** $C\\ne {A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- **II.** $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- **III.** $C\\ne {A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186237920 :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -3 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $C\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -3 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $C\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186237921 :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- **II.** $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- **III.** $C={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- **II.** $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- **III.** $C={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186237921 :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true. As a result, $C={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true. As a result, $C={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186237922 :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $B={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $B={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186237922 :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $B={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -5 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $B={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -5 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186237923 :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- **II.** $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- **III.** $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- **II.** $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- **III.** $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [:db/cas 17592186237923 :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186237927 :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,-1).$" "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,-1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186237927 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,-1)=0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,-1)=0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237930 :question/problem "$[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186237930 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-18)\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 0 & (-18)\\cdot 0+9\\cdot 1 \\\\ 0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0 & 0\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-36+0 & 0+9 \\\\ 0+0 & 0+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-18)\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 0 & (-18)\\cdot 0+9\\cdot 1 \\\\ 0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0 & 0\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-36+0 & 0+9 \\\\ 0+0 & 0+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237931 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237936 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237936 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 9-3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 9-3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18-18 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237937 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237937 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237938 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-12+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-12+12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237939 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237939 :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$" "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,0\\,|=0\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237940 :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237940 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1(-2) & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(6)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1(-2) & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(6)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237941 :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237941 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-1)(3) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(4)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$" "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-1)(3) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(4)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186237950 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=4 \\\\ 5x=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=4 \\\\ 5x=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 0-5\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237951 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=5 \\\\ 4x-y=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=5 \\\\ 4x-y=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237952 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=3 \\\\ x-y=-7.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=3 \\\\ x-y=-7.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237953 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=3 \\\\ -4x+3y=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=3 \\\\ -4x+3y=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237954 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x+3y=1 \\\\ -2x-4y=-3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x+3y=1 \\\\ -2x-4y=-3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186237990 :question/problem "If\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$F=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthen $EF=$" "If\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$F=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthen $EF=$"] [:db/cas 17592186237990 :question/worked-solution "Here, $E$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix and $F$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $E$ and $4$ rows in $F,$ so $E$ and $F$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{F}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={EF}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $EF,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,2=1\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 18 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,18=1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 18 \\\\ 15 & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,15=3\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 18 \\\\ 15 & 9\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,9=3\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$EF=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 18 \\\\ 15 & 9\\end{aligned}].$" "Here, $E$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix and $F$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $E$ and $4$ rows in $F,$ so $E$ and $F$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{F}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={EF}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $EF,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,2=1\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 18 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,18=1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 18 \\\\ 15 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,15=3\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 18 \\\\ 15 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,9=3\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$EF=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 18 \\\\ 15 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186238011 :question/problem "Find $ST$,  if  $S=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" "Find $ST$,  if  $S=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186238011 :question/worked-solution "Here, $S$ is a $1\\times 4$ matrix and $T$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $S$ and $4$ rows in $T,$ so $S$ and $T$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{T}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={ST}_{1\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $ST,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24 & ∗\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,24=2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24 & 1\\end{aligned}], &  & \\,1=2\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$ST=[\\begin{aligned}24 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Here, $S$ is a $1\\times 4$ matrix and $T$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $S$ and $4$ rows in $T,$ so $S$ and $T$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{T}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={ST}_{1\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $ST,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,24=2\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=2\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$ST=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186238054 :question/problem "$[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]=$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186238054 :question/worked-solution "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10\\cdot 1 & -10\\cdot (-5) & -10\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 6\\cdot 1 & 6\\cdot (-5) & 6\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 0\\cdot 1 & 0\\cdot (-5) & 0\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 8\\cdot 1 & 8\\cdot (-5) & 8\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 50 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -30 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -40 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10\\cdot 1 & -10\\cdot (-5) & -10\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 6\\cdot 1 & 6\\cdot (-5) & 6\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 0\\cdot 1 & 0\\cdot (-5) & 0\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 8\\cdot 1 & 8\\cdot (-5) & 8\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 50 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -30 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -40 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186238078 :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y+x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3 & 2y+x+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3=0 \\\\ 2y+x+3=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=3-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3-2x)+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 6-4x+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 9-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $y=3-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3-2(3)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,-3).$ \n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,-3).$" "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y+x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y-3 & 2y+x+3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3=0 \\\\ 2y+x+3=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=3-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3-2x)+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 6-4x+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 9-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $y=3-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3-2(3)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,-3).$ \n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,-3).$"] [:db/cas 17592186238112 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186238138 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 2